 Key conventions:
 
 - **The workspace quota: the SANDBOX measures, the facade enforces (#245/#538).** `filestore.workspace_quota` (0 ⇒ unlimited) bounds ONE workspace. The size comes from asking the sandbox — `disk_usage` for the total, `size_of` for the file being overwritten — NOT from the durable snapshot (the periodic mirror is deliberately additive, `_writeback(delete=False)`, so a snapshot-based number misses bytes the agent just created AND keeps charging for ones it deleted: that was #538) and NOT from a `path -> size` map in app memory (that made the figure a property of whichever pod answered, so two pods disagreed and the overshoot multiplied by pod count; it also leaked, twice). `WorkspaceFiles` caches only the total, for `usage_window` (5s), and the **mirror sweep publishes it** (`SandboxSync.on_measured` → `record_measurement`) so the traversal never lands on a user's request; measuring on request survives only as the cold-start fallback for a pod with no session, and an unreachable sandbox DEGRADES to the durable number rather than 500ing. It counts everything the file tree shows — including `node_modules/`/`.venv/`, which really occupy the disk even though the mirror declines to persist them (filtering them out left the file tree and the usage bar disagreeing, i.e. #538 in reverse) — but NOT the infra area beside the workspace (`.ready`, the per-sandbox `.home`), which never appears in the file tree and which `delete_file` cannot address — charging for those would mean telling a user their workspace is full of bytes they have no way to remove. Enforcement lives in `WorkspaceFiles._ensure_headroom`, the chokepoint EVERY write shares, and one `WorkspaceFull` handler in `create_app` makes it a **507** everywhere — do NOT re-check the quota per route (that is how copy/move stayed ungated, and how the route's own stale rule later refused shrinks on a full workspace). The rule is **growth**, not "already over": shrinks, same-size replaces and deletes always pass, so an over-quota workspace can be tidied up. Gate WHOLE operations at their boundary — a folder copy, `replace`, staging a run's inputs all check once up front (`ensure_room_for`) because a gate in the middle of a loop leaves half of it applied; `move` is exempt (net-zero) and `write_record` is exempt (it records an effect that ALREADY happened — refusing it only makes the re-run repeat the side effects). There is no second size cap — a size-based reaper was capacity management dressed as a policy limit, and idle reap already reclaims the whole item dir, `.home` included. A turn is refused OUTRIGHT when the workspace is already full (`chat_send.send` / `workflow_exec.drive_turn`, before the user's message is persisted): gating each write still let the agent burn a turn rediscovering it, and clearing space needs no agent because deleting is never gated.
-- **The file tree is a PRUNED preload plus lazy folders; pruned is not hidden (`docs/plan-lazy-file-tree.md`).** `GET /tree` walks with `sync/ignore.py:TREE_PRUNE` (the directory patterns of `DEFAULT_IGNORES` + `dist/`, `build/`) and `TREE_MAX_ENTRIES`: a pruned folder is LISTED and not entered, and comes back in `unwalked`; the FE draws it collapsed and asks `?prefix=<dir>&depth=1` on expand (one TanStack query per open lazy folder, `qk.treeDir`). A workspace with `node_modules/` had 12k entries at ~3 NFS round trips each (measured: 2.81 stat/entry), and the whole tree was refetched on every turn end — 50 s, per turn. The one traversal is `sandbox/walk.py:walk_tree` (breadth-first, budget checked BETWEEN directories, `os.scandir` for the real sandbox and `NfsTreeFileStore.tree`; a byte-identical copy lives in `sandbox-host`), driven by three optional `walk` kwargs that every existing caller omits — the mirror and the quota still see everything. **`TREE_PRUNE` is a THIRD meaning of the ignore list and a separate constant on purpose**: `DEFAULT_IGNORES` also decides what is backed up and what the schedule index takes instructions from, so `dist/` in it would stop backing up build outputs and silence their schedules. Two rules follow the pruned listing: "not in the list" no longer means "gone" — `fileTree.ts:presenceOf` answers `present | absent | unknown` (unknown under any unwalked ancestor) and the shell closes tabs / drops recents only on `absent`; and one path is one question — `GET /files/exists?path=` (`FileService.exists`) instead of listing the workspace to scan for it. The production idle-then-reopen cost has a SECOND part this does not touch: the host's rsync restore of the whole archive (no excludes, and locked that way — a reaped environment must come back whole).
+- **The file tree is a PRUNED preload plus lazy folders; pruned is not hidden (`docs/plan-lazy-file-tree.md`).** `GET /tree` walks with `sync/ignore.py:TREE_PRUNE` (the directory patterns of `DEFAULT_IGNORES` + `dist/`, `build/`) and `TREE_MAX_ENTRIES`: a pruned folder is LISTED and not entered, and comes back in `unwalked`; the FE draws it collapsed and asks `?prefix=<dir>&depth=1` on expand (one TanStack query per open lazy folder, `qk.treeDir`). A workspace with `node_modules/` had 12k entries at ~3 NFS round trips each (measured: 2.81 stat/entry), and the whole tree was refetched on every turn end — 50 s, per turn. The one traversal is `sandbox/walk.py:walk_tree` (breadth-first, budget checked BETWEEN directories, `os.scandir` for the real sandbox and `NfsTreeFileStore.tree`; a byte-identical copy lives in `sandbox-host`), driven by three optional `walk` kwargs that every existing caller omits — the mirror and the quota still see everything. **`TREE_PRUNE` is a THIRD meaning of the ignore list and a separate constant on purpose**: `DEFAULT_IGNORES` also decides what is backed up and what the schedule index takes instructions from, so `dist/` in it would stop backing up build outputs and silence their schedules. Two rules follow the pruned listing: "not in the list" no longer means "gone" — `fileTree.ts:presenceOf` answers `present | absent | unknown` (unknown under any unwalked ancestor) and the shell closes tabs / drops recents only on `absent`; and one path is one question — `GET /files/exists?path=` (`FileService.exists`) instead of listing the workspace to scan for it. The production idle-then-reopen cost has a SECOND part this does not touch: the host's restore of the whole archive (no excludes, and locked that way — a reaped environment must come back whole); a reap leaves one packed copy beside the tree that the next reopen extracts instead of copying path by path, valid only until the next write-back (`docs/plan-archive-pack.md`).
 - **A lifecycle sweeper is pod-local or a pure producer — never the work itself (#804).** `api/lifecycle.py` loops may touch only THIS pod's sandbox sessions / scratch (`idle_killer`, `mirror_sweeper`) or list what to ask for and enqueue it for a worker (`cluster_sweeper`, `code_sync_sweeper`, `reflect_sweeper` — each lists the `Collection` table, small by construction, and touches nothing heavier). Whole-table reads of anything that grows with content, model calls, or a walk of every collection/page are a job on a worker — the OOM this convention comes from was `cluster_sweeper` reading every `ClusterMember` and embedding every candidate on EVERY API pod's timer, with zero traffic, until the pod died. "Idempotent, so every pod can run it" is N× the full read, not free: when N pods must not all scan (or all ask), take a per-window `ScanLease` on the trigger ledger (`workflow/triggers.py`) so one pod does it and the rest skip the tick — its window is an epoch-millisecond START, never `now // interval`, because the ledger outlives the config and a raised interval would otherwise lock every pod out for good; and a timer that enqueues forever must bound its own rows (`_prune_finished_sweeps`). Boot-time seeds `store` and hand the doc to the index queue; they do not chunk + embed on the API. A pure-producer pod (`run_consumers=False`) also does NOT `aclose()` the coordinators at shutdown — `aclose` starts a consumer "so it still flushes", which on that pod would run every pending job as it exits. Three loops are neither and stay for a stated reason: `goal_offhours_sweeper` (it starts turns, and the turn engine only exists here), `notification_delivery_sweeper` (an indexed, batched relay to the outbound channel), and `turn_reclaim_sweeper` (plan-graceful-shutdown P3; app chat only — the KB send opens no claim), which runs TURNS — it lists the open `turn-claim` rows (bounded by turns in flight), judges them per conversation key, takes the orphaned ones by CAS and re-runs them through `ChatSendService.rerun` on this pod, because a turn can only run on an API pod. The claim is the only evidence of "owed" — it is deleted when the reply persists, and reading the thread's shape instead dropped owed claims on a queued Q1/Q2, the #624 notice and a peer-answered follow-up; `RECLAIM_MAX_RERUNS` bounds the re-runs. A turn checks `turn_claims.is_mine` before persisting so a copy the epoch cancelled after a takeover writes nothing and broadcasts no cancel. `blob_gc_sweeper` used to be one too — "specstar's reconcile builds the live set from the REGISTERED models only, and only the API's spec registers all of them" — until it OOMed an API pod right after `blob-gc: won lease`; it is a pure producer now (`ScanLease` → `BlobGcCoordinator.enqueue_reconcile`) and the registry problem is solved the other way round: the `blob-gc` worker boots the API's OWN composition (`__main__.build_app`, never served; `worker.API_REGISTRY_JOBTYPES`), so the registries are equal by construction, and every ask carries the asker's model names so a runner that lacks any REFUSES the pass (`_check_registry` — FAILED + a log naming them) rather than deleting their blobs. The scanned set is NOT "the models with a `Binary` field": specstar compiles a runtime collector for any `list` / `dict` / union / `Optional` field whatever its value type (only an all-scalar struct is skipped), so it is nearly every model, registered all over `create_app` — never hand-mirror it into `build_bundle`.
 - **Sandbox is created lazily** by the agent's `exec` tool on first use (grill-me Q10 "a2+" policy). Pure file operations go through FileStore and never spin one up.
 - **Workspace files are SHARED-DIR, not affinity-bound (#345).** The local sandbox keys each item to a FIXED dir `{sandbox.root}/{item_id}/root/` on a shared RWX scratch volume, so any replica resolves the same live files by id (`create(spec, sandbox_id=item)` is idempotent; `handle_for_id`/`peek_handle` derive the handle WITHOUT a session; `WorkspaceFiles._warm` PROBES the dir and falls back to the durable FileStore snapshot only when cold). This is what cures "data appears/disappears" on multi-pod — correctness no longer depends on nginx sticky routing (now just a warm-cache optimisation). The FileStore is the durable archive (recycle write-back + blob GC). Recycle (`registry.kill_idle`) rmtrees a shared dir ONLY when GLOBALLY idle — gated by a cross-pod activity heartbeat (`api/sandbox_activity.py`, an opaque `_SandboxActivity` row per item); a pod-locally-idle-but-globally-active item just drops its local session. `mirror`'s deletion is `_versions`-gated AND now reads the globally-consistent shared dir, so it no longer deletes another pod's live files. Two vols: `sandbox.root` (scratch RWX) vs `filestore.disk_root` (durable). Cross-pod **turn cancel** is still pod-local → #349. Only `kind: local`; http/mock unaffected.
